    "media:imagetext": "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nFirst East-to-West Atlantic Crossing -\nOn April 12, 1928, Hermann Koehl and his two-man\nAfter a 37-hour flight, the \"Bremen\" set down safely\ncrew set out on a mission that many believed was\nat 12:35 p.m. on April 13.\nimpossible. Aboard the Junkers W-33 \"Bremen,\" a\nsingle-engine, all-metal aircraft, the trio planned to\nThe full-color cachet depicts the \"Bremen\" in flight\nfly the Atlantic from east to west. At 5:38 a.m., they\nover the Atlantic. The map above the plane traces the\ntook off from Baldonnel Field near Dublin, Ireland.\nroute of the crossing, from Ireland to the northeast\ncoast of Newfoundland and finally to Greenly Island.\nMany attempts had been made to fight the treach-\nerous air currents hindering an East-to-West Cross-\ning. The Bremen was the first aircraft to succeed,\ndespite poor weather and poor visibility. The small\nplane's fuel was nearly exhausted when the crew\nfinally spotted Greenly, an islet off the coast of Labra-\ndor, where they could make a forced landing.\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was issued\nby Germany in 1979. It pictures the Junkers W-33\n\"Bremen\" flying across the Atlantic from Europe to\nNorth America.\nPast mark Tris C2, 1e84, spearived ry Dubie tres.\neast-to-west Atlantic crossing.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinadin S.A.\n056. 029",
